---
description: Inline code comments must be short (1-2 sentences); only doc comments (JSDoc/TSDoc/KDoc/JavaDoc) may go into full detail
alwaysApply: true
---

# Comments: concise inline, thorough doc comments

An inline comment (one sitting next to a line/block inside a function body)
must be understandable from its first sentence. Default to one sentence; add
a second only if the first genuinely can't stand alone. If a "why" needs
5-10 sentences to justify a one-line decision, that's prose, not a comment —
state the conclusion, not the deliberation that led to it.

- Don't narrate what the code obviously does (already covered by the
  making_code_changes rule — this rule is about comments that *do* carry
  real information, but bury it in length).
- Don't write comments as an essay defending a choice. One clause naming the
  reason beats a paragraph re-deriving it.
- If a comment genuinely needs more than 2 sentences to be useful (a
  subtle bug history, a link to an issue/README with the full story), that's
  fine — but that's the exception, not the default.

## Example

### Wrong
//  Guards the publish step below against a real failure mode, not a
//  hypothetical one: when "Run tests" fails during `test:e2e:prepare`
//  (fixture seeding, manifest generation) — BEFORE Playwright itself
//  ever starts — no `playwright-report/` directory is produced at
//  all. `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages` doesn't degrade gracefully when
//  its `publish_dir` is missing: it logs an ENOENT, concludes this
//  must be a "first deployment," and unconditionally runs `git
//  checkout --orphan gh-pages` — which then fails outright ("a
//  branch named 'gh-pages' already exists") on every run after the
//  very first successful one. That turned one real, attributable
//  failure (the seeding bug) into two confusing, misdirected ones in
//  the same job.
//
//  Deliberately FAILS this step (not a silent skip) when the report
//  is missing — a corrected first draft: the initial version of this
//  guard only wrote `exists=false` and let the next step's `if:`
//  quietly skip it, no failed/red step anywhere to explain why the
//  report never got published. That's the exact "verification
//  silently skips instead of failing loudly" pattern this repo's own
//  rules warn against — even though the JOB overall still failed via
//  "Run tests" (this step changes nothing about that), a skipped
//  step gives no explicit, attributable signal of its own. `exit 1`
//  with an `::error::` annotation makes this step itself show red,
//  pointing straight back at "Run tests" as the real cause, instead
//  of leaving a quiet gap for someone to puzzle over later.

### Right
//  `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages` crashes with a misleading "gh-pages
//  already exists" error if `publish_dir` is missing (e.g. "Run
//  tests" failed before Playwright started) — this step fails
//  loudly instead of silently skipping. See frontend/tests/README.md's
//  dated entry for the full story, including a corrected first draft.

```typescript
// We decided to use a Map here instead of a plain object because objects in
// JavaScript have some quirks with prototype chain lookups, and also because
// we wanted O(1) lookups without worrying about key collisions with built-in
// Object.prototype properties like "toString" or "hasOwnProperty", which
// could happen if a user-supplied key matched one of those names.
const cache = new Map();
```

```typescript
// Map avoids prototype-pollution collisions (e.g. a key named "toString").
const cache = new Map();
```

## Example: link out instead of inlining the whole story

```yaml
# `peaceiris/actions-gh-pages` crashes with a misleading "gh-pages already
# exists" error if `publish_dir` is missing (e.g. "Run tests" failed
# before Playwright started) — this step fails loudly instead of
# silently skipping. See frontend/tests/README.md's dated entry for the
# full story, including a corrected first draft.
```

Not a 20-line comment re-deriving the root cause, the failed alternative,
and the reasoning for the fix — that belongs in the linked README's dated
entry (where this repo already keeps that kind of history), not repeated
inline every time someone opens the file.

## Exception: doc comments (JSDoc/TSDoc, KDoc, JavaDoc)

A doc comment on a public function/class/type documents an API, not a line
of code — it's fine, even expected, for it to be thorough: what it does, why
it exists, how to use it, common misuse, examples, limitations.

```typescript
/**
 * Resolves a hashed refresh-token cookie to its session.
 *
 * @throws {SessionExpiredError} if the session was revoked or expired.
 * @example
 * const session = await resolveSession(req.cookies.get("rt"));
 */
```

Applies to comments in any language in this repo (`//`, `#`, `/* */`, etc.),
not just TypeScript.